        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "The image contains a single problem. Let me work through the solution.\n\n**Problem analysis:**\n\nGiven: $a_1 = 3$, and $\\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{n} = \\dfrac{a_n}{n+1} + \\dfrac{1}{n(n+1)}$.\n\n**(1)** Multiply both sides by $n(n+1)$:\n$$(n+1)a_{n+1} = n a_n + 1$$\n\nSo $(n+1)a_{n+1} - n a_n = 1$, a constant. Let $b_n = n a_n$; then $b_{n+1} - b_n = 1$, so $\\{b_n\\}$ is an arithmetic sequence with common difference 1, first term $b_1 = 1\\cdot a_1 = 3$.\n\nThus $n a_n = 3 + (n-1)\\cdot 1 = n + 2$, giving $a_n = \\dfrac{n+2}{n} = 1 + \\dfrac{2}{n}$.\n\n**(2)** $f(x) = \\sum_{k=1}^{m} a_k x^k$, so $f'(x) = \\sum_{k=1}^{m} k a_k x^{k-1} = \\sum_{k=1}^{m} (k+2) x^{k-1}$.\n\nAt $x = -2$: $f'(-2) = \\sum_{k=1}^{m} (k+2)(-2)^{k-1}$.\n\nLet $S = \\sum_{k=1}^{m} (k+2)(-2)^{k-1}$. Write $k+2 = (k-1) + 3$:\n$$S = \\sum_{k=1}^{m}(k-1)(-2)^{k-1} + 3\\sum_{k=1}^{m}(-2)^{k-1}$$\n\nUsing $\\sum_{k=1}^{m}(k-1)r^{k-1} = \\frac{d}{dr}\\left(\\frac{r^m-1}{r-1}\\cdot r\\right)$... let me compute directly with the standard formula. Let $j=k-1$, $j$ from 0 to $m-1$:\n$$S = \\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}(j+2)(-2)^j = \\sum_{j=0}^{m-1} j(-2)^j + 2\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}(-2)^j$$\n\nWith $r=-2$: $\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1} r^j = \\frac{1-r^m}{1-r} = \\frac{1-(-2)^m}{3}$.\n\nAnd $\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1} j r^j = \\frac{r - m r^m + (m-1)r^{m+1}}{(1-r)^2}$. With $r=-2$, $(1-r)^2 = 9$:\n- $r = -2$\n- $-m r^m = -m(-2)^m$\n- $(m-1)r^{m+1} = (m-1)(-2)^{m+1} = -2(m-1)(-2)^m$\n\nSo numerator $= -2 - m(-2)^m - 2(m-1)(-2)^m = -2 - (3m-2)(-2)^m$.\n\nThus $\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1} j(-2)^j = \\frac{-2-(3m-2)(-2)^m}{9}$.\n\nThen:\n$$S = \\frac{-2-(3m-2)(-2)^m}{9} + 2\\cdot\\frac{1-(-2)^m}{3} = \\frac{-2-(3m-2)(-2)^m + 6 - 6(-2)^m}{9}$$\n$$= \\frac{4 - (3m-2+6)(-2)^m}{9} = \\frac{4-(3m+4)(-2)^m}{9}$$\n\nLet me verify with $m=1$: $f'(x)=a_1=3$, so $f'(-2)=3$. Formula: $\\frac{4-(3+4)(-2)}{9}=\\frac{4+14}{9}=2$. That's wrong — let me recheck.\n\nFor $m=1$: $S = \\sum_{k=1}^{1}(k+2)(-2)^{k-1} = 3\\cdot 1 = 3$. My formula gives 2, so there's an error. Let me redo.\n\n$\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1} j r^j$ with $m=1$: only $j=0$ term $=0$. Formula: $\\frac{-2-(3\\cdot1-2)(-2)^1}{9} = \\frac{-2-1\\cdot(-2)}{9}=\\frac{0}{9}=0$. ✓\n\n$2\\sum_{j=0}^{0}(-2)^j = 2\\cdot 1 = 2$. So $S = 0+2 = 2$?? But direct gives 3.\n\nAh, I mis-split. $k+2$ with $j=k-1$: $k = j+1$, so $k+2 = j+3$, not $j+2$. Let me redo.\n\n$$S = \\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}(j+3)(-2)^j = \\sum_{j=0}^{m-1} j(-2)^j + 3\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}(-2)^j$$\n\n$m=1$: $0 + 3\\cdot 1 = 3$ ✓.\n\n$$S = \\frac{-2-(3m-2)(-2)^m}{9} + 3\\cdot\\frac{1-(-2)^m}{3} = \\frac{-2-(3m-2)(-2)^m + 9 - 9(-2)^m}{9}$$\n$$= \\frac{7 - (3m-2+9)(-2)^m}{9} = \\frac{7-(3m+7)(-2)^m}{9}$$\n\nCheck $m=1$: $\\frac{7-(10)(-2)}{9}=\\frac{7+20}{9}=3$ ✓.\n\nCheck $m=2$: $a_1=3, a_2=2$. $f'(x)=3+2\\cdot2x=3+4x$, $f'(-2)=3-8=-5$. Formula: $\\frac{7-(6+7)\\cdot4}{9}=\\frac{7-52}{9}=\\frac{-45}{9}=-5$ ✓.\n\nSo $f'(-2) = \\dfrac{7-(3m+7)(-2)^m}{9}$.",
        "tool_calls": [
